---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Wanderburg Silver Farming — When to Overtime (0.9.9 EA)';
const description =
  'Wanderburg silver answers: gold vs silver, when to Overtime after four bosses, when to stop and buy unlocks, and quest-board lump payouts. No shop prices or invented formulas.';
const heroSrc = '/images/ss_036989f9c5ee7df8dc8dbbbd7f22b5e9109dda31.1920x1080.jpg';
const ogImage =
  'https://wanderburgguide.site/images/ss_036989f9c5ee7df8dc8dbbbd7f22b5e9109dda31.1920x1080.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogImage} canonicalPath="/silver-farming/" shell="parchment">
  <div class="guide-layout">
    <div class="parchment-panel">
      <img
        class="hero-img"
        src={heroSrc}
        alt="Wanderburg camp hub / shop still"
        width="1920"
        height="1080"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <p class="hero-caption">Silver buys the loadout. Overtime pays the silver.</p>

      <h1>Silver Decision Tree</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="text-align:center;">
        Gold vs silver, when to Overtime, when to cash out and buy, and quest lumps.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gold-vs-silver">Don’t mix gold and silver — what’s silver for?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Gold</strong> is in-run (eat villages, flocks, enemies → level-ups). <strong>Silver</strong> is meta — you spend it between runs on the loadout: modules, crew, vehicles, captains.
      </p>
      <p>
        Creators sometimes say “gold” while standing in the shop. Follow the UI word <strong>silver</strong> for meta spend. Mortar “gold collection records” are in-run gold — not a silver route.
      </p>
      <p>
        Steam store copy never names silver or shop prices. Official about-text only says unlocks for modules, vehicles, artifacts, and captains happen between runs. Unlock toast in a run only lets you <strong>purchase it in the loadout</strong> — silver pays that buy. What to buy once you’re rich: <a href="/best-modules/">Best modules</a> · <a href="/captains/">Captains</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Gold</th><th>Silver</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>When</strong></td>
              <td>During the run — eat villages, flocks, enemies</td>
              <td>Meta — Overtime, quests, end-of-run bank</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Buys</strong></td>
              <td>Level-ups and in-run upgrades</td>
              <td>Loadout: modules, crew, vehicles, captains</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="overtime">Four bosses down — do I enter Overtime?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Yes, if you can still kite</strong> — Overtime is the primary silver loop after you clear the map’s four bosses.
      </p>
      <p>
        The UI offers end screen <strong>or</strong> Overtime for extra silver / beat a record. Enemies speed up and “go crazy.” Players say the longer you last, the better the silver feel — we don’t have a formula, so don’t greed a dead run.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cash out instead if you’re one hit from death, a boss is still on the field (grind tapes call that “not great”), or your kit has no mobility. Speed / nitro / ram help. Empress half-speed is an OT risk — see <a href="/captains/">Captains</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="decision-tree">
        <h3>Quick call</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Enter OT</strong> — HP fine, field clear, you can kite.</li>
          <li><strong>Cash out</strong> — brick mid-fight, leftover boss, or zero mobility.</li>
          <li><strong>Map rule</strong> — farm a map you can full-clear + OT. Dusky Pines is spoken as difficulty 3 with a <strong>1.3× silver</strong> tag; that tag does nothing if you never reach OT. Wasteland gets called hardest — skip until your kit survives.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="when-to-buy">How long do I farm silver before I stop and buy unlocks?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Answer:</strong> Stop when you’ve got a toast waiting and enough silver to actually purchase it — don’t sit on unlocks while you grind “one more” OT for no reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        Early on, <strong>8 silver</strong> can’t buy anything. One long stream called an Overtime payout around <strong>~3,000</strong> “enough for a proper upgrade”; a grind end-screen cited <strong>3k silver</strong> as “enough for one day.” Those are single-run color, not a price table — use them as “you’re in buy-range,” not a target formula.
      </p>
      <p>
        Priority buys once the toast exists: Front Cannon (side cannons 10), Arms (ram 10), Turret Layer / Side Ballista if you’re on that family, Repair Crew after 100 health boxes. No verified shop price grid — we won’t invent one. Kits that survive OT long enough to bank: turrets+arrows (drop and retreat), mines+ram (keep moving), cannons+mortar once built. Mobility first.
      </p>

      <h2 id="quests">Quest board — when do I grab the lump silver?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Answer:</strong> Knock out the lifetime / one-run quests while you play normally — the lump pays when the counter fills, not on a special farm map.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Consume 100 units in one run</strong> → <strong>500 silver</strong>. <strong>Consume 1,000 sheep overall</strong> → <strong>2,500 silver</strong> (spoken as meta progression). Route pastures and flocks while you clear bosses; don’t derail a clean OT just to chase sheep if you’re already dying.
      </p>
      <p>
        Unlock quests matter too: destroy <strong>100 health boxes overall</strong> → Repair Crew; destroy <strong>120 walking enemies overall</strong> → Companion (then still buy). Daily quests show up on the HUD; reset rules aren’t clear — don’t plan a schedule around them yet.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Quest</th><th>Pays / unlocks</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Consume <strong>100 units in one run</strong></td><td><strong>500 silver</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Consume <strong>1,000 sheep overall</strong></td><td><strong>2,500 silver</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Destroy <strong>100 health boxes overall</strong></td><td>Unlock <strong>Repair Crew</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Destroy <strong>120 walking enemies overall</strong></td><td>Unlock <strong>Companion</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Don’t</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Cash out at four bosses with full HP and a clear field.</li>
        <li>Chase Demo 8 mage-tower farm clips.</li>
        <li>Treat in-run gold records as silver rates.</li>
        <li>Quote shop prices from a stream — numbers aren’t crossed.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="crosslinks">
        <strong>Next:</strong>
        <a href="/best-modules/">Best modules</a> ·
        <a href="/captains/">Captains</a> ·
        <a href="/">Home</a>
      </div>

      <details class="still-checking">
        <summary>Still checking</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Overtime formula (start rate, step-up, cap) — not published.</li>
          <li>Shop price grid — no data worth printing.</li>
          <li>Daily quest reset rules — no data.</li>
          <li>0.9.9 patch string not confirmed on Steam news.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>

    <aside class="guide-side" aria-label="Quick tips">
      <div class="side-panel">
        <h2>Do This Now</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Gold = in-run. Silver = shop.</strong></li>
          <li>Clear <strong>four bosses</strong>, then Overtime if you can kite.</li>
          <li>Stop and buy when a toast is waiting and silver covers it.</li>
          <li>Grab quest lumps (100 units / 1,000 sheep) while you clear.</li>
          <li>Farm a map you can full-clear — 1.3× is useless if you brick.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
